  canned: { sidechain: `Sidechain compression ducks the bass every time the kick hits, so they share the low end instead of fighting. Put a compressor on the bass, trigger it from the kick, and dial ~3–6 dB of gain reduction with a fast attack and a release timed to the groove. For the classic pumping pad effect, sidechain the pads to the kick too.`, mask: `That's frequency masking — two sounds fighting in the same range. Fix it three ways: high-pass everything except kick and bass around 100 Hz, carve a little dip in the bass where the kick's body sits (~60–100 Hz), and sidechain the bass to the kick. Keep both mono below ~120 Hz.`, reverb: `Try the Abbey Road trick: high-pass the reverb around 600 Hz and low-pass it near 10 kHz so it adds space without mud. Put a short pre-delay (10–20 ms on drums, 20–60 ms on vocals) to keep transients punchy, and sidechain the reverb tail to the dry signal for clarity.`, eight08: `Tune your 808 to the root of your key so it locks with the bassline — an out-of-tune 808 is the #1 beginner giveaway. Keep it mono, high-pass everything else above it, and add a touch of saturation so it still reads on phone speakers that can't reproduce the sub.`, master: `For streaming, master to around -14 LUFS integrated with a true peak under -1 dBTP — every platform normalizes, so chasing loudness just costs you dynamics. Make a separate louder -8 to -6 LUFS master only for club/DJ use. A dynamic -14 master often sounds bigger after normalization.`, chords: `Start in a minor key for that emotional EDM feel — try i–VI–III–VII. Lock everything to the scale so nothing clashes, start your bassline on the root of each chord, and loop the progression over 4 or 8 bars. Most hits use just 3–4 chords; flow matters more than complexity.`, arrange: `Write the busiest section first (usually the drop), then copy it and remove elements to build the intro and breakdown. Keep changes on 8/16/32-bar boundaries so it stays danceable. Energy management — tension then release — is the whole craft.`, ai: `Use AI as a sketch, not a finished track: batch ideas in Suno or Udio, prompt for key and tempo, export stems and MIDI, then finish the arrangement, sound design and mix in your DAW. Run AI mastering as a first pass, then trust your ears.`, def: `Great question. The short version: define your key first so nothing clashes, keep your low end mono and clean, and reference a commercial track in your genre at matched loudness. Want me to break any of those down further, or shall I point you to the right lesson?` },
